        'albani_method_source':      'Where the text comes from',
        'albani_method_source_body': 'Speech-recognition software transcribed each recording. It divides every transcript into {minutes}-minute segments and estimates who is speaking in each one. The segments locate a passage within a recording, and the speaker estimates separate a sermon from a question-and-answer session. Both can be wrong, which is why every passage links back to the recording.',
        'albani_method_scope':       'Which recordings count',
        'albani_method_scope_body':  'Only transcripts in the current format are counted. A transcript made earlier by a different method is listed but not counted until it has been redone: that earlier method could invent text where it heard none. Recordings with no transcript, and passages in English, are also left out.',
        'albani_method_spelling':    'Counting a word once, however it is spelt',
        'albani_method_spelling_body': 'Hausa can be written with or without its hooked letters (ɓ, ɗ, ƙ, ƴ), and the transcripts do both. The two spellings count as one word, as do q and k, single and double letters, and the different apostrophes. Words are not reduced to a dictionary form. Instead, the word lists name by hand every form a term takes, such as matasa, matasan and matashi.',
        'albani_method_stopwords':   'Leaving out the commonest words',
        'albani_method_stopwords_body': 'Words such as da (and), ya (he) or wannan (this) carry little meaning on their own and would fill every list. They are removed using the 76 Hausa stop words published by Bashir, Bichi and Adamu (2024), along with other grammatical words and this speaker’s habitual fillers, ainihin and wato. Religious formulae such as sallallahu alaihi wa sallam are counted separately, as whole phrases.',
        'albani_method_layers':      'Telling Hausa from Arabic',
        'albani_method_layers_body': 'The transcripts write Arabic quotations in Latin letters, so the alphabet cannot separate the two languages. A simple rule marks a passage as Arabic when it contains several Arabic grammatical words and almost no Hausa ones. The rule is approximate. Arabic words that have become part of Hausa, such as addini, shari’a and ilimi, count as Hausa.',
        'albani_method_lexicon':     'The word lists',
        'albani_method_lexicon_body': 'The youth and politics lists are a first draft based on the research questions, and a Hausa-speaking specialist will check every term. In the meantime, words with two meanings, such as ƙasa (“country” or “to fail”), are shown but kept out of the combined figures, and sarki is not counted where it refers to God, as in Allah Maɗaukakin Sarki.',
        'albani_method_glosses':     'The translations',
        'albani_method_glosses_body': 'The short translations beside the Hausa words have two sources. Those of the word-list terms belong to the draft word lists. Those of the other words, found around the terms, were proposed by an AI assistant (Claude, Anthropic) from general knowledge of Hausa, without reading the passages, and a Hausa-speaking specialist will check them. A word whose sense depends on its context, or that looks like a transcription error, is left untranslated rather than guessed.',
        'albani_method_entities':    'Finding the people and places',
        'albani_method_entities_body': 'A named-entity model trained on African languages, including Hausa (MasakhaNER, Adelani et al. 2022), read every transcript once and proposed some 23,000 mentions of people, places and organisations. A check of a random sample found most of the people and places right and half of the organisations. Those proposals were then sorted by hand into a fixed list of names and spellings, which alone is counted: the model plays no part in the figures. A spelling with another meaning is left out, as with isa (also a verb), shehu (any shaykh), maka (“for you”, which the transcription can write like Makka) or albani (the preacher or his Syrian namesake), so the counts are floors.',
        'albani_method_collocates':  'Finding the words that travel together',
        'albani_method_collocates_body': 'For each term, the page looks at the {window} words on either side of it, within one speaker’s turn, and compares how often each word appears there with how often it appears everywhere else. A word is shown only when the difference is too large to be due to chance. The test used is log-likelihood (Dunning 1993), adjusted because thousands of words are tested at once (Benjamini and Hochberg 1995). Words are then ranked by how many times more frequent they are near the term, a measure called the log ratio (Hardie 2014), so a rare but telling word can rank above a common one.',
        'albani_method_rights':      'What can be quoted',
        'albani_method_rights_body': 'Every transcript contributes to the counts, but passages are quoted only from transcripts whose text is public on the IWAC website. No figure on this page reproduces more than three consecutive words from a private transcript.',
